<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      "Obriši račun" (App Store guideline 5.1.1(v): an account created in the
      app must be deletable from the app).

      ANONYMISATION, NOT ERASURE — the owner's decision. A tournament other
      people played in is shared history: deleting the rows would silently
      rewrite somebody else's results. So the PERSON disappears
      (display name, phone, avatar, in-game name, devices, personal scorepad
      history) while the tournaments, pairs and matches stay exactly as they
      were.

      This column is the whole switch. `deleted_at is not null` means:

        * the public profile 404s (PublicProfileService),
        * POST /user/me/sync REFUSES with 410 ACCOUNT_DELETED instead of
          lazily re-creating the row (SlugService.ensureProfile),
        * anywhere a name is rendered for this uid, the i18n string
          `profile.deletedUser` is rendered instead (DisplayNames).

      The ROW AND ITS SLUG ARE KEPT on purpose. Dropping the row would put the
      slug back in the pool, and the next person whose name normalises to
      `marko-markovic` would inherit every old link, QR code and shared URL
      that pointed at the deleted account. A kept row with no personal data in
      it turns those links into a clean 404 instead.

      Nullable timestamptz, no default: every existing profile is live.
      Indexed, because the lookups that matter are the negative ones — "give
      me this slug IF it is not deleted" runs on every profile page view.
      Partial index so it stays the size of the deleted set rather than of the
      whole table.

      columnExists / MARK_RAN, like the rest of this directory, so a database
      where the column was added by hand is a no-op rather than a boot failure.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-13-account-deletion" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="user_profiles" columnName="deleted_at"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <addColumn tableName="user_profiles">
            <column name="deleted_at" type="timestamptz"/>
        </addColumn>

        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="user_profiles" columnName="deleted_at"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="2026-09-13-account-deletion-index" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <and>
                <columnExists tableName="user_profiles" columnName="deleted_at"/>
                <not>
                    <indexExists indexName="idx_user_profiles_deleted_at"/>
                </not>
            </and>
        </preConditions>

        <sql dbms="postgresql">
            create index idx_user_profiles_deleted_at
                on user_profiles (deleted_at)
                where deleted_at is not null;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="postgresql">drop index if exists idx_user_profiles_deleted_at;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
